@*
    Adding a field.

    The controls change with the type, because the frozen schema's if/then rules decide which
    facets a type may carry — `decimal` needs precision and scale, `enum` needs values, `ref` needs
    an entity, and `string` may have a maxLength that `boolean` may not. Offering every facet for
    every type would be offering a descriptor the apply refuses.

    Nothing here writes to the database. The field lands in the working copy and goes through the
    same dry run as every other change.
*@
@*
    The chrome belongs to <AlvoEditor>: the right-hand modal side sheet every multi-value edit uses (spec §3.1),
    which guards what was typed, submits on Enter and Ctrl/Cmd+Enter, and cannot submit twice. A field editor that
    sat permanently under the list was a panel the operator scrolled past on every visit.
*@
<AlvoEditor TestId="field-sheet" Title="@(IsEditing ? $"Edit {Editing}" : "New field")"
            Subtitle="@(IsEditing
                ? "The change joins the working copy. Preview shows what it does to the table before anything runs."
                : "It joins the working copy. Preview shows what it does to the table before anything runs.")"
            SubmitText="@(IsEditing ? "Save to the working copy" : "Add to the working copy")" SubmitTestId="field-save" Dirty="Dirty"
            OnSubmit="Add" OnClose="Cancel">
    <ExtraActions>
        @* An entity is rarely one field short: the second action stages this one and leaves the sheet open for
           the next, instead of a reopen per field. *@
        @if (!IsEditing)
        {
            <AlvoButton data-testid="field-save-another" Busy="_another.Busy" OnClick="_ => AddAnother()">
                Save and add another
            </AlvoButton>
        }
    </ExtraActions>
    <ChildContent>
    <div class="a-stack">
        @RefusalPanel.Of(_refusal, IsEditing ? "That change cannot be saved" : "That field cannot be added")

        <Field Label="Name" For="new-field-name">
            <ChildContent>
                @* The hint carries markup, so the Field keeps it and names the box; the sheet's first field takes
                   focus when it opens (DefaultFocus), so it is typed into straight away. *@
                <MudTextField T="string" @ref="_nameField" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Immediate="true" id="new-field-name"
                              aria-describedby="new-field-name-hint" Placeholder="e.g. scheduled_for" autocomplete="off"
                              Class="a-mono"
                              Value="_facets.Name" ValueChanged="name => _facets.Name = name ?? string.Empty" />
            </ChildContent>
            <Hint>
                @if (IsEditing && !string.Equals(_facets.Name, Editing, StringComparison.Ordinal))
                {
                    <text>
                        Renaming <code class="a-mono">@Editing</code>. The change is carried by
                        <code class="a-mono">renamedFrom</code>, so the apply moves the column instead of
                        dropping it and creating a new one — the rows come with it.
                    </text>
                }
                else
                {
                    <span title="@DescriptorNames.Member">@DescriptorNames.Hint</span>
                }
            </Hint>
        </Field>

        <Field Label="Value" LabelId="new-field-kind">
            <ChildContent>
                <ChipGroup TValue="FieldKind" Items="_kinds" Selected="[_facets.Kind]" aria-labelledby="new-field-kind"
                           Label="KindWord" SelectedChanged="kinds => ChooseKind(kinds[0])" />
            </ChildContent>
            <Hint>@KindHint</Hint>
        </Field>

        @if (_facets.Kind == FieldKind.Rollup)
        {
            <Field Label="Type" LabelId="new-field-type">
                <span class="a-hint" data-testid="rollup-type">
                    Stored as <code class="a-mono">@FieldFacets.Word(_facets.RollupType)</code> —
                    @(_facets.KeepsDeclaredType ? "as declared, which holds this rollup's value."
                        : _facets.RollupOp == "count" ? "a count is a whole number." : "the type of the field it aggregates.")
                </span>
            </Field>
        }
        else
        {
            <Field Label="Type" LabelId="new-field-type">
                <ChipGroup TValue="FieldType" Items="@(_facets.Kind == FieldKind.Computed ? _facets.ComputedTypesOffered : _types)"
                           Selected="[_facets.Type]" aria-labelledby="new-field-type"
                           Label="Word" SelectedChanged="types => _facets.Type = types[0]" />
            </Field>
        }

        <div class="a-field">
            <span class="a-label" id="new-field-constraints">Constraints</span>
            <div class="a-row a-row--wrap" role="group" aria-labelledby="new-field-constraints">
                @if (_facets.RequiredOffered)
                {
                    <MudCheckBox T="bool" Value="_facets.Required" ValueChanged="on => _facets.Required = on" Label="required" />
                }
                @if (_facets.UniqueOffered)
                {
                    <MudCheckBox T="bool" Value="_facets.Unique" ValueChanged="on => _facets.Unique = on" Label="unique" />
                }

                @*
                    Offered only while the field is not unique, because a unique field already carries
                    an index — declaring a second one over the same column is a second B-tree the
                    database maintains on every write and no query can use. The Indexes tab makes the
                    same point in prose; this is the half that stops it being declarable.
                *@
                @if (!(_facets.UniqueOffered && _facets.Unique))
                {
                    <MudCheckBox T="bool" Value="_facets.Indexed" ValueChanged="on => _facets.Indexed = on" Label="indexed"
                                 data-testid="field-index" />
                }
            </div>
            @if (_facets.UniqueOffered && _facets.Unique && _facets.Indexed)
            {
                <span class="a-hint">
                    <code class="a-mono">index</code> is kept as declared and is redundant beside
                    <code class="a-mono">unique</code>, which already carries one. Clear
                    <code class="a-mono">unique</code> to change it.
                </span>
            }
        </div>

        @if (_facets.Kind != FieldKind.Rollup)
        {
            @switch (_facets.Type)
            {
                case FieldType.String:
                    <Field Label="Max length" For="new-field-max">
                        <ChildContent>
                            @* Text with the numeric keypad, read by FacetNumber: a number box reports what it cannot parse as empty. *@
                            <MudTextField T="string" @ref="_maxField" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Immediate="true" id="new-field-max"
                                          InputMode="InputMode.numeric" Placeholder="no limit"
                                          aria-describedby="@_numbers.DescribedBy(MaxBox, "new-field-max-hint")"
                                          Error="@_numbers.Has(MaxBox)"
                                          Value="@NumberText(MaxBox, _facets.MaxLength)" ValueChanged="TypeMaxLength" />
                            @_numbers.Under(MaxBox, () => _maxField!.FocusAsync())
                        </ChildContent>
                        <Hint>Leave it empty for no limit — the schema's own default.</Hint>
                    </Field>
                    break;
                case FieldType.Decimal:
                    <div class="a-field-row">
                        <Field Label="Precision" For="new-field-precision">
                            <ChildContent>
                                <MudTextField T="string" @ref="_precisionField" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Immediate="true"
                                              id="new-field-precision" InputMode="InputMode.numeric"
                                              aria-describedby="@_numbers.DescribedBy(PrecisionBox, "new-field-precision-hint")"
                                              Error="@_numbers.Has(PrecisionBox)"
                                              Value="@NumberText(PrecisionBox, _facets.Precision)" ValueChanged="TypePrecision" />
                                @_numbers.Under(PrecisionBox, () => _precisionField!.FocusAsync())
                            </ChildContent>
                            <Hint>Total digits, not the ones before the point.</Hint>
                        </Field>
                        <Field Label="Scale" For="new-field-scale">
                            <ChildContent>
                                <MudTextField T="string" @ref="_scaleField" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Immediate="true"
                                              id="new-field-scale" InputMode="InputMode.numeric"
                                              aria-describedby="@_numbers.DescribedBy(ScaleBox, "new-field-scale-hint")"
                                              Error="@_numbers.Has(ScaleBox)"
                                              Value="@NumberText(ScaleBox, _facets.Scale)" ValueChanged="TypeScale" />
                                @_numbers.Under(ScaleBox, () => _scaleField!.FocusAsync())
                            </ChildContent>
                            <Hint>Digits after the point.</Hint>
                        </Field>
                    </div>
                    break;
                case FieldType.Enum:
                    <Field Label="Values" For="new-field-values">
                        <ChildContent>
                            <MudTextField T="string" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Immediate="true" id="new-field-values"
                                          Placeholder="e.g. scheduled, in_progress, completed"
                                          aria-describedby="new-field-values-hint"
                                          Value="_facets.Values" ValueChanged="text => _facets.Values = text ?? string.Empty" />
                        </ChildContent>
                        <Hint>Comma separated. An enum with no values is refused by the schema.</Hint>
                    </Field>
                    break;
                case FieldType.Ref:
                    <Field Label="Points at" LabelId="new-field-target">
                        <ChipGroup TValue="string" Items="Targets" Selected="[_facets.Target]" aria-labelledby="new-field-target"
                                   SelectedChanged="targets => _facets.Target = targets[0]" />
                    </Field>
                    break;
            }
        }

        @if (_facets.Kind == FieldKind.Rollup)
        {
            @if (_facets.Sources.Count == 0)
            {
                <Refusal data-testid="rollup-no-source">
                    Nothing points at <code class="a-mono">@Entity</code>. A rollup aggregates the rows of an entity with a
                    ref field here — add one on the child first.
                </Refusal>
            }
            else
            {
                <Field Label="Rolls up" LabelId="rollup-from">
                    <ChipGroup TValue="string" Items="RollupFroms" Selected="[_facets.RollupFrom]" aria-labelledby="rollup-from"
                               SelectedChanged="picked => _facets.RollupFrom = picked[0]" />
                </Field>
                @foreach (var refused in _facets.Sources.Where(source => source.Refusal is not null))
                {
                    <span class="a-hint" data-testid="@($"rollup-refused-{refused.Entity}")">
                        <code class="a-mono">@refused.Entity</code> — @refused.Refusal
                    </span>
                }

                <Field Label="Operation" LabelId="rollup-op">
                    <ChipGroup TValue="string" Items="FieldFacets.RollupOps" Selected="[_facets.RollupOp]" aria-labelledby="rollup-op"
                               SelectedChanged="ops => _facets.RollupOp = ops[0]" />
                </Field>

                @if (_facets.RollupOp != "count" && _facets.Source is not null)
                {
                    <Field Label="Of the field" LabelId="rollup-field">
                        <ChipGroup TValue="string" Items="AggregatableNames" Selected="[_facets.RollupField]" aria-labelledby="rollup-field"
                                   SelectedChanged="fields => _facets.RollupField = fields[0]" />
                    </Field>
                }

                @if (_facets.Source is { Via.Count: > 1 } source)
                {
                    <Field Label="Through" LabelId="rollup-via">
                        <ChipGroup TValue="string" Items="source.Via" Selected="[_facets.RollupVia]" aria-labelledby="rollup-via"
                                   SelectedChanged="refs => _facets.RollupVia = refs[0]" />
                    </Field>
                }
            }

            @* `where` is in the frozen schema and refused by this build, so it gets the build's sentence and no box. *@
            <Refusal data-testid="rollup-where-refused">
                <code class="a-mono">where</code> — @(WhereRefusal?.Consequence ?? "a filter on the child rows is not offered here.")
            </Refusal>
            @if (_facets.DeclaresRollupFilter)
            {
                <MudCheckBox T="bool" Value="_facets.RemoveRollupFilter" ValueChanged="on => _facets.RemoveRollupFilter = on"
                             Label="Remove the declared filter" data-testid="rollup-remove-where" />
            }
        }

        @if (_facets.Kind == FieldKind.Computed)
        {
            <Field Label="Expression" For="new-field-computed">
                <ChildContent>
                    <MudTextField T="string" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Immediate="true" Lines="2" id="new-field-computed"
                                  Class="a-mono" spellcheck="false" Placeholder="e.g. unit_price * amount"
                                  aria-describedby="new-field-computed-hint"
                                  Value="_facets.Computed" ValueChanged="text => _facets.Computed = text ?? string.Empty" />
                </ChildContent>
                <Hint>
                    CEL over this row's own fields — and its rollups. No constants: the column is DDL, which carries no
                    bound value. The apply compiles it, and Preview shows its refusal in the framework's own words.
                </Hint>
            </Field>
        }

        @if (_facets.DrawsDefault)
        {
            @* A boolean's default is a group of chips, which a <label for> cannot name; the group names the
               label by its id instead. *@
            <Field Label="Default value" For="@(_facets.Type is FieldType.Boolean ? null : "new-field-default")"
                   LabelId="new-field-default-label">
                <ChildContent>
                    @if (_facets.Type is FieldType.Boolean)
                    {
                        <ChipGroup TValue="string" Items="_flags" Selected="[_facets.Default]"
                                   aria-labelledby="new-field-default-label"
                                   Label="@(option => option.Length == 0 ? "none" : option)"
                                   SelectedChanged="options => _facets.Default = options[0]" />
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <MudTextField T="string" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Immediate="true" id="new-field-default"
                                      aria-describedby="new-field-default-hint" Placeholder="@_facets.DefaultPlaceholder"
                                      autocomplete="off"
                                      Value="_facets.Default" ValueChanged="text => _facets.Default = text ?? string.Empty" />
                    }
                </ChildContent>
                <Hint>
                    What a create stores when the caller omits the field. It becomes a column
                    <code class="a-mono">DEFAULT</code> and is filled in on every write that composes a whole
                    row. Leave it empty for no default.
                </Hint>
            </Field>
        }

        @if (_facets.KeepsUndrawnDefault)
        {
            <MudCheckBox T="bool" Value="_facets.RemoveUndrawnDefault" ValueChanged="on => _facets.RemoveUndrawnDefault = on"
                         Label="Remove the declared default" data-testid="field-remove-default" />
        }

        @* Every facet this editor draws no control for, and what the save does to it. Before this, such a facet was
           either rewritten from a stale prefill or carried without a word (docs/todo-admin.md §8d item 17). *@
        @if (_facets.Notes() is { Count: > 0 } notes)
        {
            <div class="a-field" data-testid="field-notes">
                <span class="a-label">Not drawn here</span>
                @foreach (var note in notes)
                {
                    <span class="a-hint" data-testid="@($"field-note-{note.Facet}")">
                        <code class="a-mono">@note.Facet</code> <code class="a-mono">@Short(note.Value)</code> — @note.Reason
                    </span>
                }
            </div>
        }

    @* The facets the frozen schema declares and this build refuses. Without this the editor's
       silence about `default` and `validation` is indistinguishable from an oversight — the
       question every reader of this panel asks is "where is the default value", and the answer
       exists, in the framework's own words. Served verbatim (§2.3): a second wording of a refusal
       is a third spelling of one truth. *@
    @*
        Collapsed and last: the answer is there for the operator who asks where the default or the
        validation box went, and out of the way of the one adding a field — open, it took more than
        half of the dialog.
    *@
    @if (FieldRefusals.Count > 0)
    {
        <details class="a-disclosure" data-testid="refused-facets">
            <summary>@FieldRefusals.Count facet@(FieldRefusals.Count == 1 ? " is" : "s are") not supported yet</summary>
            <p class="a-section__sub">
                Declared by the schema, refused at apply — a whole facet, or the one form of it named
                here. A refused facet has no control above; a refused <em>form</em> of one does, and this
                says which value it will not take.
            </p>

            @foreach (var refusal in FieldRefusals)
            {
                <ListRow data-testid="@($"refused-{refusal.Slot}")">
                    <code class="a-mono a-listrow__key">@refusal.Slot</code>
                    <span class="a-listrow__rest a-listrow__rest--stacked">
                        <span class="a-section__sub">@refusal.Consequence</span>
                        <span class="a-section__sub">@refusal.Fix</span>
                    </span>
                </ListRow>
            }
        </details>
    }
    </div>

    </ChildContent>
</AlvoEditor>
